1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,492
Two nuclei merge into one. Somewhere else, one splits

2
00:00:03,532 --> 00:00:06,724
into two. The total count remains unchanged, but the

3
00:00:06,764 --> 00:00:09,956
biological measurement is broken.

4
00:00:13,300 --> 00:00:16,900
Across fifty held-out microscopy fields, four had the exact

5
00:00:16,940 --> 00:00:20,540
reference count, yet retained one hundred unmatched instance errors:

6
00:00:20,580 --> 00:00:24,180
fifty false positives and fifty false negatives.

7
00:00:29,300 --> 00:00:32,248
We built NucleiLens to expose the hidden assumptions behind

8
00:00:32,288 --> 00:00:35,236
every automated count.

9
00:00:39,300 --> 00:00:43,544
Instead of trusting a single black-box output, NucleiLens runs

10
00:00:43,584 --> 00:00:47,828
nine deterministic sensitivity probes. A sparse bipartite correspondence graph

11
00:00:47,868 --> 00:00:52,112
matches objects across runs, isolating count-changing splits and merges

12
00:00:52,152 --> 00:00:56,396
from simple boundary noise.

13
00:01:00,300 --> 00:01:04,898
Everything runs entirely inside your browser using WebAssembly. In

14
00:01:04,938 --> 00:01:09,536
this real field, baseline segmentation counts seventy-four nuclei. But

15
00:01:09,576 --> 00:01:14,174
look at Tile thirteen: an alternate sensitivity run also

16
00:01:14,214 --> 00:01:18,812
counts seventy-four, yet contains opposing split and merge alternatives.

17
00:01:23,300 --> 00:01:27,444
Reviewers inspect competing outlines and confirm a sensitivity alternative.

18
00:01:27,484 --> 00:01:31,628
Candidates remain hypotheses. Conflicting overlaps are rejected, fresh IDs

19
00:01:31,668 --> 00:01:35,812
are assigned, and edits can be undone.

20
00:01:45,300 --> 00:01:48,716
Export an unsigned thirty-two-bit label TIFF and a JSON

21
00:01:48,756 --> 00:01:52,172
audit linking the input, configuration, edits, and mask hash.

22
00:01:52,212 --> 00:01:55,628
Human choices remain unvalidated.

23
00:02:00,300 --> 00:02:04,452
Marker-controlled watershed runs NumPy, SciPy, and scikit-image in a

24
00:02:04,492 --> 00:02:08,644
dedicated browser worker. Uploaded images stay on your device.

25
00:02:08,684 --> 00:02:12,836
Hosting and library downloads still use the network.

26
00:02:23,300 --> 00:02:27,724
We evaluated NucleiLens across all fifty held-out test images

27
00:02:27,764 --> 00:02:32,188
of the Broad Institute's BBBC039 benchmark. The pipeline achieves

28
00:02:32,228 --> 00:02:36,652
a mean count absolute error of 5.12 and an

29
00:02:36,692 --> 00:02:41,116
instance F1 score of 0.827.

30
00:02:45,300 --> 00:02:49,324
Crucially, our review queue prioritizes where human attention is

31
00:02:49,364 --> 00:02:53,388
needed most. At a twenty-percent review budget, object disagreement

32
00:02:53,428 --> 00:02:57,452
captures 45.8% of all annotated errors, more than double

33
00:02:57,492 --> 00:03:01,516
random review. And when our graph-ranking hypothesis fell short

34
00:03:01,556 --> 00:03:05,580
at 39.5%, we reported the negative result and chose

35
00:03:05,620 --> 00:03:09,644
the stronger queue.

36
00:03:13,300 --> 00:03:17,092
NucleiLens is an open-source prototype for research and education.

37
00:03:17,132 --> 00:03:20,924
Chromium and Firefox workflows are tested. Human time savings

38
00:03:20,964 --> 00:03:24,756
and clinical use are unvalidated.

39
00:03:28,300 --> 00:03:32,580
Don't just count nuclei. See beyond the count. NucleiLens.
